is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — that VLAN 10 hosts (172.16.10.x, specifically pc5) cannot reach any VLAN 30 hosts (172.16.30.x, the "design floor") — is not substantiated by any evidence in the live lab. Exhaustive probing of the entire inter-VLAN path finds every component functioning correctly.

On mswitch0 (the multilayer switch the user called "the big switch"), both SVI interfaces are up: vlan10@br0 is operational at 172.16.10.254/24 (802.1Q id 10) and vlan30@br0 is operational at 172.16.30.254/24 (802.1Q id 30). IP forwarding is enabled, and there are zero iptables or ebtables rules that could block traffic. The routing table carries both subnets as directly connected. Switch1 correctly bridges VLAN 10 access hosts to mswitch0 via tagged subinterfaces eth0.10 and eth1.10. Switch3 correctly bridges VLAN 30 access hosts to mswitch0 via tagged subinterface eth0.30. Ping tests from pc5 to all three VLAN 30 hosts (pc12, pc13, pc14) returned 0% loss in both the forward and reverse direction. pc5 can reach both its own gateway and the VLAN 30 SVI directly. mswitch0's ARP neighbour table shows pc5 as REACHABLE on vlan10 and pc12 as REACHABLE on vlan30.

The inter-VLAN routing infrastructure is intact, correctly configured, and passing traffic normally. The user's report describes a fault that does not exist. No corrective action is required.
